\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Top row shows injected waveform and waveform reconstruction produced with the neutrino model PCs at 150\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}kpc in three ET and two Voyager detectors. Bottom row shows Omega Scan spectrograms of the data from the detector in the configuration with the highest SNR. Bottom left plot shows the signal is clearly visible by eye for a 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}kpc injection, while the bottom right plot shows almost nothing visible at 150\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}kpc. This waveform was confidently classified at 150\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}kpc as corresponding to the neutrino model with g-mode and SASI emission, even though at that distance those features are clearly not visible by eye in the noisy Omega scan.}}{10}{}}
\newlabel{fig:recon}{{5}{10}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {B}Minimum SNR}{10}{}}
\newlabel{subsec:minsnr}{{VI\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}B}{10}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Minimum detectable SNR for each classification statement. All injections performed in a simulated A+ configuration that included AdVirgo and Kagra. The top two plots both pertain to mechanism classification, and the bottom two are for g-modes and SASI. All results are organized such that positive Bayes values correspond to correct classifications regardless of whether the feature is present or not.}}{11}{}}
\newlabel{fig:min_snr}{{6}{11}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {C}Mechanism Classification}{11}{}}
